<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vedanta Desika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1269–<a href="/years/1370">1370</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Vedanta Desikan, Veṅkaṭanātha, Thoopul Nigamantha Desikan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> Desika was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnava</a> guru, philosopher and poet of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> who wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Manipravalam, Tamil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, and is revered as the most important guru of the Vadakalai sect. Born at Tūppul in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, he studied until the age of 20 under his maternal uncle Ātreya Rāmānuja, of the lineage of disciples that began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>. More than 100 works are attributed to him, among them the Tattvamuktākalāpa and the Śatadūṣaṇī, besides 862 verses in 22 metres on the deities of temples. He shaped Vishishtadvaita Vedanta by joining Purva Mimamsa to Vedanta, and made prapatti, complete surrender to God, a path to liberation of its own, open to all.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the account of a Sri Vaishnava temple chronicle, when Ulugh Khan, the future Muhammad bin Tughluq, sacked the temple of Srirangam in 1323, some 12,000 scholars and temple functionaries were killed; the teacher Pillai Lokacharya carried the processional image south, and <b>Vedanta Desika</b> hid the sacred manuscripts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.83,79.71&amp;year=1334" aria-label="Kanchipuram on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6738 1686 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vedanta Desika"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7188 1956h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7188 1956h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.83,79.71&amp;year=1334">What was here in 1334? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
